From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Fri, 9 Sep 1994 21:25:03 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-84] POL: Independence Initiatives

>From: dkrieger@netcom.com (Dave Krieger)
>Date: Thu, 8 Sep 1994 13:14:15 -0700
>Subject: [#94-9-63] POL: You say you want a revolution; well, you know...
>
>I'm ::nosending to the list a proposed ballot initiative authored in part
>by J. Neal Schulman (author of _The Rainbow Cadenza_, _Alongside Night_,
>and other libertarian classics) for secession from the Union.

Neil sent us copies already, which I've read.  Pretty good job.

>...Does anyone
>on the list know much about the signature-gathering process for ballot
>initatives in general, and California in particular?

Whaddaya wanna know?  It'll take about 1.2 million valid signatures to get it
on the CA ballot, as I understand it.  The more you gotta pay for, the more
expensive it'll get.  The other thing is that you've got about 180 days after
you file your declaration of intent to petition with the State before you
have to get your signatures in.

>...I know already that
>you're pretty much dead in the water without the paid assistance of a
>specialized petition-circulating company, but couldn't find any in the
>Yellow Pages.

You don't necessarily need paid petitioners.  It depends upon how much
popular support there is for the issue.  If you do need the pros, though,
then you may need to be able to pay $.25 to $1.00 per valid signature.
And the reason why you won't find 'em in the Yellow Pages is that there's
only a couple statewide organizations.  Tom Arnold, a longtime libertarian,
used to run one out of his place in San Diego.  He's one of the guys that
Perot consulted with in his petitioning effort.  Don't have his number,
but you could call up SD info and ask them.  For some reason I seem to
recall his having moved to Colorado, but no more details than that.

Then there's also John Robertson, a veteran of many LP ballot access petition-
ing drives.  Last I heard, he could be reached through Tamara Clark in Las
Vegas, NV - but I've heard she's moving, too, so better get moving!

I like Neil's initiative, but I don't think CA is the place to try it first.
CA's too dominated by expatriate Easterners in political outlook.  In
addition to the other states I mentioned in my earlier post, I'd also guess
that Alaska and Hawaii would be good bets to pass such a thing.

